  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front is progressing its way through NY and PA this\nafternoon. Across NY, cloud cover and showers have kept\ntemperatures cooler and not allowed for much heating. Clouds\nhave cleared in the Wyoming valley in NEPA so there is starting\nto be some surface based CAPE to develop. A bowing storm in\ncentral PA is on line with moving into the Wyoming Valley\nbetween 4 and 5 pm. As of now, strong wind gusts look to be the\nmain concern as the storms move through NEPA. Shear is strong,\nwith mesoanalysis showing about 50 knots of bulk shear ahead of\nthe line. \n\nIn NY, there is a thin line of showers along the front with some\ngusts of 30 to 35 mph so still will see some breezy conditions\nwith its passage but nothing severe. \n\nTonight into tomorrow, strong cold air advection will drop\ntemperatures into the 30s and 40s along with 20 to 30 mph winds\ngusts. Wind chills will be at or below freezing for much of the\nday so quite the difference from the recent weather we have been\nhaving. Temperatures Saturday night will result in the first\nwidespread freeze as lows fall into the 20s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAn area of high pressure will remain present over the region \nthroughout next week. A quasi-stationary low pressure system \nwill develop along the East Coast and linger off the Carolinas\nand mid Atlantic for much of the week. The strong ridge in place\nwill keep the low off shore with any precipitation remaining\neast of our region. Temperatures will gradually warm up into \nearly next week, becoming more seasonable. Sunny skies may help \npush high temperatures back into the 70s in the valleys.",
